1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,880
Spin a globe, and Earth
looks like it's melting.

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:06,650
South America. Africa.

3
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:07,740
India.

4
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:09,940
Greenland. Florida.

5
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:12,430
Baja. Kamchatka.

6
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,640
Every point aims south.

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,410
Like somebody left the planet
on the dashboard in July.

8
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,040
Two-thirds of all land
is north of the equator.

9
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,650
So are nearly nine in ten of us.

10
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:28,810
You've probably heard those southern
points are just a coincidence.

11
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:31,670
They're not. Not entirely.

12
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,680
So why do the continents point south?

13
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:38,170
By the end, you'll know what
they're really pointing at.

14
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:43,400
Along the way: why you can't dig
to China from anywhere in America.

15
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:47,080
A theory from eighteen seventy-five
that explained all of it,

16
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,787
and was dead wrong.

17
00:00:48,827 --> 00:00:53,080
And the last one: the four continents
your country might crash into.

18
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:54,570
Eventually.

19
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:58,040
Bad news: you're not the first to notice.

20
00:00:58,080 --> 00:00:59,860
Not even close.

21
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:04,820
In sixteen twenty, Francis Bacon wrote
that the Old World and the New are both

22
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:07,590
'narrow and pointed toward the south.'

23
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:12,700
And in the same breath, he said Africa and
South America's matching capes were 'not

24
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,350
to be attributed to mere accident.'

25
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:20,150
Then he wrote down an open question:
is there even a southern continent down

26
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:24,100
there? Nobody would lay eyes on
Antarctica for two more centuries.

27
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,670
Remember that.

28
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:29,900
Now, you may have heard that
Bacon predicted continental drift.

29
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:31,150
He didn't.

30
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:35,767
He wasn't snapping Africa and South
America together like puzzle pieces.

31
00:01:35,807 --> 00:01:39,320
He was saying they're
shaped alike, side by side.

32
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:43,200
So Bacon saw the drip,
but never tried to explain it.

33
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,800
For centuries, nobody else could.

34
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:48,170
That did not stop them from trying.

35
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:51,940
Captain Cook's naturalist,
Johann Reinhold Forster,

36
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:54,900
claimed that every great
southern tip looks alike:

37
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:58,560
high, rocky, the end of a mountain chain.

38
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:03,920
His explanation? A giant flood came
roaring in from the south-west and carved

39
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:08,240
every one of them. Some of the top
scientists of the day bought it.

40
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:11,010
Geology later asked for a refund.

41
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:15,600
By the eighteen-forties, the most famous
scientist alive was calling the tips

42
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:20,080
'pyramidal.' And someone had written
down almost the exact list you saw at the

43
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:21,250
start.

44
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,260
Greenland, Baja, Florida,
India, Kamchatka.

45
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,150
'All their points turned
towards the south.'

46
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:33,160
Then, in the nineteen-seventies,
the pattern got its name,

47
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,480
as a joke. A magazine writer
called it 'continental drip':

48
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,300
a fake theory you could prove
just by staring at a map.

49
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:45,740
Three years later, a geologist
published his own version of the joke.

50
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:49,190
Hold on to him. He matters
more than you'd think.

51
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:51,860
But the shapes are just the first clue.

52
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:54,950
The next one is on the
far side of the planet.

53
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:57,500
Start at the top of the world.

54
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:00,620
The North Pole isn't land, it's ocean.

55
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,300
Which makes Santa's workshop,
technically, a houseboat.

56
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:08,220
Stand on the ice there, and the sea floor
is about two and a half miles beneath your

57
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:09,630
boots.

58
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,420
Now go to the bottom.

59
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:14,940
At the South Pole, you're
standing almost two miles up,

60
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:18,430
on a sheet of ice more than
a mile and a half thick.

61
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,900
The two ends of the planet
are photographic negatives.

62
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:25,470
Ocean on top. A continent at the bottom.

63
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:27,900
And they're almost the same size.

64
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:33,150
Drag the Arctic Ocean down onto
Antarctica, and the sizes nearly match.

65
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:35,813
But here's the part
that doesn't make sense.

66
00:03:35,853 --> 00:03:37,280
They don't just match.

67
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:42,400
They line up. Most of Antarctica sits
directly opposite the Arctic Ocean,

68
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,090
on the exact other side of the Earth.

69
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:47,700
And it's not just the poles.

70
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:51,620
Almost everywhere you look,
land has ocean on its far side,

71
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:56,720
not more land. Which brings us to
a classic American playground myth.

72
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:00,080
If you dig straight down,
you'll reach China.

73
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:04,560
Let's test it. Start in Kansas, right
at the center of the lower forty-eight,

74
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,267
and dig. Ignore everything on
the way down that would kill you,

75
00:04:08,307 --> 00:04:09,740
and keep going.

76
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:12,460
You come out in the southern Indian Ocean.

77
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:17,630
The nearest land is a tiny French island,
more than two hundred miles away.

78
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:20,860
Not China. Not even the right hemisphere.

79
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:26,270
In fact, there isn't a single spot in the
United States that sits opposite China.

80
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:31,313
Even if you started in China, three times
out of four you'd surface in the ocean.

81
00:04:31,353 --> 00:04:33,900
The people who can actually dig to China?

82
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,630
Argentinians.

83
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:39,680
Now flip every continent to the far
side of the planet and lay it over the

84
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:44,850
original. Only about one-eighth of
the land has land on the other side.

85
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:49,170
That's less than half of what you'd expect
if the continents had been sprinkled at

86
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:50,210
random.

87
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:53,840
So try a puzzle. Slice
the Earth into two halves,

88
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:58,380
any way you like, and cram as much
land as you can into one of them.

89
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:01,020
The winner is centered on western Europe.

90
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:03,633
It scoops up most of the world's land.

91
00:05:03,673 --> 00:05:06,270
And it's still more than half water.

92
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:11,910
There is no way to cut this planet in
half and get a half that's mostly land.

93
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:13,880
Now flip the globe over.

94
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:18,360
The other half is eighty-nine percent
water, centered in the empty Pacific near

95
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:19,690
New Zealand.

96
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:23,700
The Pacific alone could swallow
every continent on Earth,

97
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:25,300
with room to spare.

98
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:27,510
Yes, even Asia.

99
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,920
Ocean on top, land at the
bottom, land avoiding land.

100
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:35,747
This planet is strangely lopsided.

101
00:05:35,787 --> 00:05:39,050
And the drip might be
just the part you can see.

102
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:43,640
And back in eighteen seventy-five, one
man thought he'd found a single shape that

103
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:45,240
explained all of it.

104
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:47,210
And it wasn't a ball.

105
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:52,010
Before we meet him: every Myth Juice video
takes one thing you were taught about the

106
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,680
world and checks it
against the real planet.

107
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,410
Want the next one?

108
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:57,290
Subscribe.

109
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,747
His name was William Lowthian Green.

110
00:06:00,787 --> 00:06:02,240
He wasn't a professor.

111
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:05,650
He was the foreign minister
of the Kingdom of Hawaii.

112
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:09,707
And he'd been obsessed with those
pointy southern tips for years.

113
00:06:09,747 --> 00:06:13,500
He even demonstrated them with
a bent card dipped in water.

114
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:15,820
Remember those 'pyramidal' tips?

115
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:18,540
Green thought the whole
planet was heading that way:

116
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:23,180
cooling, shrinking, and slowly
collapsing toward a tetrahedron.

117
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:25,320
A four-cornered pyramid.

118
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:27,160
It sounds ridiculous.

119
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:29,200
But watch what it explained.

120
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:31,640
Most land in the north: check.

121
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:35,890
Three continents ending in
points aimed south: check.

122
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:39,170
An ocean on top of the world: check.

123
00:06:39,210 --> 00:06:41,970
A continent at the bottom: check.

124
00:06:42,010 --> 00:06:45,010
Land sitting opposite water: check.

125
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:47,460
One shape, every clue.

126
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:52,830
And it caught on. By eighteen ninety-nine,
a leading geologist was telling Britain's

127
00:06:52,870 --> 00:06:57,890
top geographers that a shrinking Earth
had to squeeze toward a tetrahedron.

128
00:06:57,930 --> 00:07:02,930
He even pointed out that most of North
America sits opposite the Indian Ocean.

129
00:07:02,970 --> 00:07:05,710
The same ocean you just dug into.

130
00:07:05,750 --> 00:07:10,270
By nineteen eighteen, a children's
magazine was telling kids that one day,

131
00:07:10,310 --> 00:07:12,590
the Earth would turn into a pyramid.

132
00:07:12,630 --> 00:07:14,480
Sleep tight, kids.

133
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:19,520
So, hang on. If it explained all
that, how do we know it's wrong?

134
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:23,970
Because the whole idea needed
a cooling, shrinking Earth.

135
00:07:24,010 --> 00:07:28,690
And critics could measure the planet:
a ball, slightly squashed at the poles.

136
00:07:28,730 --> 00:07:33,490
Not a pyramid. And by then,
scientists had discovered radioactivity,

137
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:36,270
quietly heating Earth from the inside.

138
00:07:36,310 --> 00:07:40,270
Over the next few decades,
the shrinking engine stalled.

139
00:07:40,310 --> 00:07:42,790
The pyramid vanished from geology.

140
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:44,670
Green's checklist was real.

141
00:07:44,710 --> 00:07:47,760
He'd just pinned the clues
on the wrong suspect.

142
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:52,670
The real suspect had been hiding
in plain sight on every world map.

143
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:54,830
A few people had guessed it before.

144
00:07:54,870 --> 00:07:58,190
It took one stubborn man
to make the case out loud.

145
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:01,120
And his story ends on one of the drips.

146
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:06,590
Around nineteen ten, a young German
weather scientist named Alfred Wegener

147
00:08:06,630 --> 00:08:08,470
wrote to his future wife.

148
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:11,910
Doesn't South America fit
against Africa, he asked,

149
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:14,280
as if they had once been joined?

150
00:08:14,330 --> 00:08:17,540
'This is an idea I'll have to pursue.'

151
00:08:17,690 --> 00:08:22,290
In nineteen twelve, he stood up before a
meeting of geologists and told them the

152
00:08:22,330 --> 00:08:26,660
continents move. Then a
world war buried his book.

153
00:08:26,730 --> 00:08:30,130
Once the world read it,
the reaction was brutal.

154
00:08:30,170 --> 00:08:33,890
One society president called
it 'utter, damned rot.'

155
00:08:33,930 --> 00:08:37,170
In Germany, he never landed
a full professorship.

156
00:08:37,210 --> 00:08:40,580
It took a university in
Austria to give him one.

157
00:08:40,650 --> 00:08:45,930
And critics had one killer question:
what could possibly push a continent?

158
00:08:45,970 --> 00:08:49,170
Wegener guessed Earth's spin, and tides.

159
00:08:49,210 --> 00:08:50,990
Both were far too weak.

160
00:08:51,030 --> 00:08:52,950
He admitted it himself.

161
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:56,400
'The Newton of drift theory
has not yet appeared.'

162
00:08:56,570 --> 00:09:00,850
In nineteen thirty, Wegener was
deep on the ice of Greenland.

163
00:09:00,890 --> 00:09:03,140
One of the drip's own tips.

164
00:09:03,290 --> 00:09:07,570
On his fiftieth birthday, he left a camp
in the middle of the ice sheet with a

165
00:09:07,610 --> 00:09:10,500
Greenlander named Rasmus Villumsen.

166
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:13,100
Neither of them made it back.

167
00:09:14,050 --> 00:09:18,730
They found Wegener's body the next
May, carefully buried in the snow.

168
00:09:18,770 --> 00:09:21,100
Villumsen was never found.

169
00:09:21,450 --> 00:09:24,290
Wegener never saw his idea proven.

170
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:29,010
The proof was hiding on the ocean
floor: stripes of magnetized rock,

171
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:31,650
mirrored on both sides
of the mid-ocean ridges,

172
00:09:31,690 --> 00:09:34,990
like a tape recording of
new seafloor being made.

173
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:40,160
The paper that decoded those stripes came
out thirty-three years after he died.

174
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:45,680
Today, satellites clock the continents
moving at about the speed your fingernails

175
00:09:45,830 --> 00:09:50,240
grow. Or your toenails, depending
on which government agency you ask.

176
00:09:50,390 --> 00:09:52,110
So Wegener was right.

177
00:09:52,150 --> 00:09:54,750
But that still doesn't explain the drip.

178
00:09:54,790 --> 00:09:58,560
Why would so many of them
end up pointing the same way?

179
00:09:58,630 --> 00:10:03,920
Run the clock backwards, and the big drips
all start pointing at the same place.

180
00:10:04,670 --> 00:10:07,270
Back into the age of dinosaurs.

181
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:11,030
Watch the southern continents
slide home: South America,

182
00:10:11,070 --> 00:10:14,040
Africa, India, Australia.

183
00:10:14,090 --> 00:10:18,317
They lock together into one
supercontinent: Gondwana.

184
00:10:18,357 --> 00:10:20,770
And at its heart sits Antarctica.

185
00:10:20,810 --> 00:10:23,380
Geologists call it the keypiece.

186
00:10:23,550 --> 00:10:25,830
Remember Bacon's open question?

187
00:10:25,870 --> 00:10:28,110
Is there even a southern continent?

188
00:10:28,150 --> 00:10:31,400
The drips were pointing
at it the whole time.

189
00:10:32,070 --> 00:10:34,270
Now let the clock run forward.

190
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:39,840
Cracks open, and new ocean starts
seeping in between Africa and Antarctica.

191
00:10:39,890 --> 00:10:43,050
Then the South Atlantic
unzips from the bottom up,

192
00:10:43,090 --> 00:10:45,530
splitting Africa from South America.

193
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:47,690
The south tears open first.

194
00:10:47,730 --> 00:10:52,630
The last seam, near the equator,
hangs on for millions of years more.

195
00:10:52,670 --> 00:10:54,950
And then everyone moves out.

196
00:10:54,990 --> 00:10:57,830
Except Antarctica. It barely budges.

197
00:10:57,870 --> 00:10:59,720
It keeps the house.

198
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:03,270
India sprints: up to
about eight inches a year,

199
00:11:03,310 --> 00:11:06,550
several times faster
than Africa or Australia.

200
00:11:06,590 --> 00:11:09,670
For a continent, that's reckless driving.

201
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:12,200
Then it slams into Asia.

202
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:16,150
Here's the explanation a geologist
will most likely give you.

203
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:21,560
Put the pieces back together, and their
pointy ends all sat close to Antarctica.

204
00:11:21,710 --> 00:11:25,590
As they drifted apart, they
roughly kept their orientation.

205
00:11:25,630 --> 00:11:29,240
So those points still aim
back to where they tore away.

206
00:11:29,510 --> 00:11:31,830
The big drips aren't drips at all.

207
00:11:31,870 --> 00:11:33,680
They're scars.

208
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:36,550
Back to that second drip joke.

209
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:39,720
The geologist who wrote
it was John Holden.

210
00:11:39,770 --> 00:11:44,130
Writing for a science humor journal,
he declared that every continent is a

211
00:11:44,170 --> 00:11:48,500
teardrop, with the pointed end
showing the direction it came from.

212
00:11:48,550 --> 00:11:54,190
Drips always flow down, he reasoned,
so our maps must be upside down.

213
00:11:54,230 --> 00:11:59,750
He was joking. But one of the most famous
photos of Earth ever taken really was shot

214
00:11:59,790 --> 00:12:01,710
with Antarctica on top.

215
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:03,760
It was published flipped.

216
00:12:03,810 --> 00:12:07,500
So by Holden's logic,
it was right the first time.

217
00:12:07,650 --> 00:12:11,743
And one day, he wrote, the continents
would splash back together into a

218
00:12:11,783 --> 00:12:13,850
supercontinent called 'Pangooy.'

219
00:12:13,890 --> 00:12:16,330
On a Tuesday, at nine in the morning.

220
00:12:16,370 --> 00:12:18,700
He did not say which time zone.

221
00:12:19,270 --> 00:12:24,910
Here's the twist. Six years earlier,
the very same John Holden had helped map,

222
00:12:24,950 --> 00:12:28,080
for real, how Pangaea broke apart.

223
00:12:28,250 --> 00:12:31,250
His joke even cited his own real work.

224
00:12:31,290 --> 00:12:34,170
That line about the point
showing where it came from?

225
00:12:34,210 --> 00:12:35,543
Not just a punchline.

226
00:12:35,583 --> 00:12:37,780
It was basically the answer.

227
00:12:38,530 --> 00:12:40,490
And the joke wasn't done.

228
00:12:40,530 --> 00:12:43,450
Geologists have since found real drips.

229
00:12:43,490 --> 00:12:48,010
Under Nevada, a cold blob of rock
appears to be sinking into the Earth,

230
00:12:48,050 --> 00:12:50,090
like honey off a spoon.

231
00:12:50,130 --> 00:12:54,210
One of the scientists who found it
admitted he'd been 'anti-drip' since his

232
00:12:54,250 --> 00:12:55,660
early days.

233
00:12:55,930 --> 00:13:00,530
Now, the honest part, because we're
not going to fake you a tidy answer.

234
00:13:00,570 --> 00:13:02,510
Not everything drips.

235
00:13:02,550 --> 00:13:05,230
Australia's Cape York points north.

236
00:13:05,270 --> 00:13:10,190
So do Mexico's Yucatán, Denmark's
Jutland and Siberia's Taymyr.

237
00:13:10,230 --> 00:13:13,070
And the Antarctic Peninsula has no choice.

238
00:13:13,110 --> 00:13:16,480
From the South Pole,
every direction is north.

239
00:13:16,550 --> 00:13:19,190
Smaller drips have their own stories.

240
00:13:19,230 --> 00:13:23,870
Several million years ago, the Pacific
Plate grabbed Baja and started dragging it

241
00:13:23,910 --> 00:13:25,790
away from mainland Mexico.

242
00:13:25,830 --> 00:13:27,450
It's still going.

243
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:29,530
Florida isn't a drip at all.

244
00:13:29,570 --> 00:13:34,170
It's the top of a gigantic
limestone platform, miles thick,

245
00:13:34,210 --> 00:13:37,477
built up by sea life
in warm, shallow water.

246
00:13:37,517 --> 00:13:42,410
And under all that limestone sits bedrock
that started out next to West Africa.

247
00:13:42,450 --> 00:13:46,700
Like a lot of people in Florida,
it's originally from somewhere else.

248
00:13:46,770 --> 00:13:50,490
And Africa's real southern tip
isn't the Cape of Good Hope.

249
00:13:50,530 --> 00:13:55,450
It's Cape Agulhas. The name means
'needles,' and the best-known story says

250
00:13:55,490 --> 00:13:59,130
that's because sailors' compass
needles there pointed true north.

251
00:13:59,170 --> 00:14:03,450
So, if that story is right, Africa's
southern tip is named after pointing

252
00:14:03,490 --> 00:14:04,700
north.

253
00:14:04,770 --> 00:14:07,210
So is the drip just a coincidence?

254
00:14:07,250 --> 00:14:11,970
The big ones are most likely not: they're
scars from a supercontinent that tore

255
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:13,850
apart around Antarctica.

256
00:14:13,890 --> 00:14:17,930
The small ones have no single answer,
and some of it may just be us,

257
00:14:17,970 --> 00:14:19,660
connecting dots.

258
00:14:19,730 --> 00:14:21,930
And the rest of the lopsided planet?

259
00:14:21,970 --> 00:14:25,797
The land piled up in the north,
the land that avoids land?

260
00:14:25,837 --> 00:14:28,890
There's no clear sign of
a deep force arranging it.

261
00:14:28,930 --> 00:14:33,530
The likeliest answer: it's a snapshot of
where the drifting pieces happen to be

262
00:14:33,570 --> 00:14:35,020
right now.

263
00:14:35,690 --> 00:14:37,810
So where is the drip going?

264
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:42,670
Since Bacon wrote his line, the Atlantic
has grown about a school bus wider.

265
00:14:42,710 --> 00:14:47,230
Give it a couple hundred million years,
and geologists think a new supercontinent

266
00:14:47,270 --> 00:14:50,670
will form. They just can't
agree on what it looks like.

267
00:14:50,710 --> 00:14:52,810
Here are the four main models.

268
00:14:52,850 --> 00:14:58,410
One: the Atlantic slams shut, and America
crashes back into Africa in about two

269
00:14:58,450 --> 00:15:00,330
hundred fifty million years.

270
00:15:00,370 --> 00:15:02,470
Pangaea Proxima.

271
00:15:02,510 --> 00:15:08,230
Two: the Pacific closes instead,
and the Americas hit Antarctica first.

272
00:15:08,270 --> 00:15:12,050
Novopangaea. One team's favorite bet.

273
00:15:12,090 --> 00:15:17,450
Three: the Arctic Ocean vanishes,
and America meets Asia practically at the

274
00:15:17,490 --> 00:15:20,180
North Pole. Amasia.

275
00:15:20,230 --> 00:15:25,230
Four: both oceans close at once,
and Australia ends up in the middle of

276
00:15:25,270 --> 00:15:29,840
everything. Australia
plus America: Aurica.

277
00:15:29,890 --> 00:15:35,530
Africa, Antarctica, Asia or Australia:
which neighbor are you betting on?

278
00:15:35,570 --> 00:15:37,780
One word, in the comments.

279
00:15:37,890 --> 00:15:41,890
In a way, Holden's continental
splash really is coming.

280
00:15:41,930 --> 00:15:43,900
Just not on a Tuesday.

281
00:15:44,050 --> 00:15:48,010
One study ran the climate on one
of these future supercontinents.

282
00:15:48,050 --> 00:15:52,700
Only a tenth to a sixth of the land
would stay livable for mammals.

283
00:15:52,770 --> 00:15:54,700
Reminder: you're a mammal.

284
00:15:55,050 --> 00:15:59,170
Whichever future wins,
the map we know gets torn up.

285
00:15:59,210 --> 00:16:03,140
The drip is just a fingerprint
of where the pieces have been.

286
00:16:03,210 --> 00:16:08,050
Four hundred years ago, Bacon said the
matching capes of Africa and South America

287
00:16:08,090 --> 00:16:11,090
were no accident. Looks like he was right.

288
00:16:11,130 --> 00:16:12,610
It wasn't an accident.

289
00:16:12,650 --> 00:16:14,340
It was a breakup.

290
00:16:14,590 --> 00:16:19,560
Every map you've ever seen is
one frame of a very slow movie.

291
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:22,730
And the next frame is already being shot.

292
00:16:22,770 --> 00:16:26,890
Right now in Ethiopia,
three cracks meet in a giant Y,

293
00:16:26,930 --> 00:16:30,540
and one of them is slowly
unzipping East Africa.

294
00:16:30,610 --> 00:16:33,450
What happens if Africa splits in two?

295
00:16:33,490 --> 00:16:34,943
That's our next story.

296
00:16:34,983 --> 00:16:37,450
And if you want to watch
the map keep changing,

297
00:16:37,490 --> 00:16:38,780
subscribe.
